---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Option from "../components/Option.astro";
import Calculator from "../components/Calculator.astro";
import CodeFile from "../components/CodeFile.astro";

import hostConfig from "../../examples/02-shared-host/host/wrangler.jsonc?raw";
import hostIndex from "../../examples/02-shared-host/host/src/index.js?raw";
import appConfig from "../../examples/02-shared-host/app/wrangler.jsonc?raw";
import appIndex from "../../examples/02-shared-host/app/src/index.js?raw";
import isolatedConfig from "../../examples/02-shared-host/app/wrangler.isolated.jsonc?raw";
import isolatedIndex from "../../examples/02-shared-host/app/src/isolated.js?raw";
import previewWorkflow from "../../examples/ci/preview.yml?raw";

const repo = "https://github.com/yomna-shousha/durable-objects-previews";
const docs = "https://developers.cloudflare.com";
---

<Base
	title="Durable Objects and Worker Previews: keep your namespace count flat"
	description="Why every Preview of a Worker with Durable Objects uses namespaces, how fast that adds up, and the patterns that keep the count flat. Tested, with runnable examples."
>
	<main>
		<div class="eyebrow">Cloudflare Workers · Durable Objects · Previews</div>
		<h1>Durable Objects and Worker Previews: keep your namespace count flat</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Each Durable Object class uses one namespace in production and another in every Preview. If every pull
			request gets a Preview, the count grows with your open PRs until you hit the account limit and deploys fail.
			This page explains how the count works and compares the patterns that avoid it. Every result here was
			measured, and every example is runnable.
		</p>

		<ul class="toc">
			<li><a href="#tldr">TL;DR</a></li>
			<li><a href="#counting">How namespaces are counted</a></li>
			<li><a href="#calculator">Calculator</a></li>
			<li><a href="#options">Options</a></li>
			<li><a href="#compare">Comparison</a></li>
			<li><a href="#setup">Set up the shared host</a></li>
			<li><a href="#measured">What we measured</a></li>
			<li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>A Worker that defines Durable Object classes gets one namespace per class, in production and in
				every Preview.</strong> Namespaces are created at deploy time, even for classes the Preview never calls.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>To stop Previews from adding namespaces, move the classes into a separate Worker that you never
				preview.</strong> Bind to it with <code>script_name</code>. In our test, Previews of the app Worker
				created <strong>zero</strong> new namespaces.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>For PRs that change Durable Object code, use an isolated Preview.</strong> A CI path filter keeps
				that cost to the PRs that need it.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Delete Previews when PRs close.</strong> Their namespaces are freed right away.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="counting">How namespaces are counted</h2>
		<p>
			A <a href={`${docs}/durable-objects/`}>Durable Object</a> namespace is one class in one Worker. When you run
			<code>wrangler preview</code>, Cloudflare
			<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/resources/#durable-objects`}>gives the Preview its own namespace</a> for each
			class, so Previews never read or overwrite each other's state. Those namespaces count toward the same account
			limit as production. That limit is
			<a href={`${docs}/durable-objects/platform/limits/`}>500 on Workers Paid and 100 on Free</a>.
		</p>
		<div class="formula">namespaces = classes × (1 production + number of Previews)</div>
		<p>
			Namespaces are named <code>&lt;worker&gt;_&lt;Class&gt;</code> in production and
			<code>&lt;worker&gt;_&lt;preview&gt;_&lt;Class&gt;</code> in a Preview. A Worker with 3 classes and 150 open
			PR Previews uses <strong>453 namespaces</strong> on its own.
		</p>
		<div class="callout warn">
			<p>
				<strong>Unused classes still count.</strong> A Preview gets a namespace for every class in your
				migrations, whether or not the branch's code uses it. In our test, a class that nothing ever called still
				got its own namespace in production and in each Preview.
			</p>
		</div>

		<h2 id="calculator">Calculator</h2>
		<p>Plug in your numbers to compare the three workable options below.</p>
		<Calculator />

		<h2 id="options">Options</h2>

		<Option
			id="option-a"
			letter="A"
			title="Isolated Previews (the default)"
			cost="classes × each Preview"
			tone="warn"
			verdict="Fine for a few PRs"
		>
			<p>
				Keep the classes in your Worker and run <code>wrangler preview</code> for each PR. This is what the
				<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/resources/#durable-objects`}>Previews docs</a> describe. Each Preview
				gets fresh, empty Durable Object storage and runs the branch's Durable Object code.
			</p>
			<p>
				Example: <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples/01-per-preview`}><code>examples/01-per-preview</code></a>
			</p>
			<ul slot="pros">
				<li>Full isolation, with no shared state.</li>
				<li>Tests the branch's Durable Object code.</li>
				<li>Zero setup.</li>
			</ul>
			<ul slot="cons">
				<li>Cost grows with every open PR.</li>
				<li>Adding a Durable Object class multiplies the cost.</li>
			</ul>
		</Option>

		<Option
			id="option-b"
			letter="B"
			title="Shared Durable Object host"
			cost="fixed: classes × 2"
			tone="good"
			verdict="Recommended for many PRs"
		>
			<p>
				Move the Durable Object classes into their own Worker, the <em>host</em>, and deploy it only from
				<code>main</code>. Your app Worker has no classes and no migrations. It binds to the host with
				<code>script_name</code>. Previews of the app create no namespaces.
			</p>
			<p>
				Deploy two copies of the host: one for production and one for Previews (staging). Previews then never
				touch production state. Inside the staging host, each Preview keeps its state separate by prefixing
				object names with its PR number.
			</p>
			<p>
				Example: <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples/02-shared-host`}><code>examples/02-shared-host</code></a>
				· <a href="#setup">Setup guide</a>
			</p>
			<ul slot="pros">
				<li>Namespace count stays flat no matter how many PRs are open.</li>
				<li>
					<a href={`${docs}/workers/versions-and-deployments/version-urls/`}>Version URLs</a> work on the app
					Worker, because it no longer defines a Durable Object.
				</li>
				<li>No production state is reachable from Previews.</li>
			</ul>
			<ul slot="cons">
				<li>Previews run the host's <em>deployed</em> Durable Object code, not the branch's.</li>
				<li>Previews share one staging namespace per class, so isolation relies on key prefixes.</li>
				<li>
					Data a Preview writes stays in the staging host after the Preview is deleted. Expire it with an alarm or
					clean it up in a job.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</Option>

		<Option
			id="option-c"
			letter="C"
			title="Shared host, isolated when Durable Object code changes"
			cost="classes × 2 + classes × each DO-changing PR"
			tone="good"
			verdict="Best of both"
		>
			<p>
				Use option B by default. When a PR changes Durable Object code, CI deploys an isolated Preview instead. It
				uses a second Wrangler config whose entry point also exports the classes. Only those PRs pay for namespaces.
			</p>
			<p>
				The isolated config targets a separate Worker (<code>my-app-isolated</code>), so its migrations never
				touch the production app Worker. That Worker is created automatically on its first Preview.
			</p>
			<p>
				Example: <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/02-shared-host/app/wrangler.isolated.jsonc`}><code>wrangler.isolated.jsonc</code></a>
				and the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/ci/preview.yml`}>CI workflow</a>
			</p>
			<ul slot="pros">
				<li>Low, predictable cost.</li>
				<li>Durable Object changes are still tested in isolation.</li>
			</ul>
			<ul slot="cons">
				<li>Two Wrangler configs to keep in sync.</li>
				<li>Isolated Previews use a different hostname (<code>pr-N-my-app-isolated…</code>).</li>
			</ul>
		</Option>

		<h3>Patterns that don't help</h3>

		<Option
			id="option-separate-workers"
			letter="✕"
			title="A separate Worker per PR"
			cost="classes × each PR"
			tone="bad"
			verdict="Not recommended"
		>
			<p>
				Deploying <code>my-app-pr-123</code> as its own Worker, with a name suffix or a Wrangler environment, costs
				the same per PR as option A. You also have to clean up the Workers yourself. Switch to
				<code>wrangler preview</code> to keep everything under one Worker and get built-in cleanup.
			</p>
		</Option>

		<Option
			id="option-version-urls"
			letter="✕"
			title="Version URLs on the Worker that defines the classes"
			cost="n/a"
			tone="bad"
			verdict="Not available"
		>
			<p>
				<a href={`${docs}/workers/versions-and-deployments/version-urls/#limitations`}>Version URLs are not generated</a>
				for Workers that implement a Durable Object. They also run against production resources, so they would
				share production state. Once you move the classes out (option B), version URLs work on the app Worker.
			</p>
		</Option>

		<Option
			id="option-script-name"
			letter="✕"
			title="Point a Preview's bindings back at production"
			cost="classes × each Preview"
			tone="bad"
			verdict="Still creates namespaces"
		>
			<p>
				Adding <code>script_name</code> to <code>previews.durable_objects.bindings</code> doesn't stop
				namespace creation while the same Worker still exports the classes. We tested this, and the Preview still
				got its own namespaces. The classes have to live in a different Worker.
			</p>
		</Option>

		<h3>Also worth doing</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Delete Previews when PRs close</strong> with
				<code>wrangler preview delete --name pr-123</code>. Their namespaces are freed right away. A
				<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/#limits`}>Worker can have up to 500 Previews</a>. When it reaches that,
				the least recently deployed Preview is deleted automatically, but that cap is higher than most accounts'
				namespace limit.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Merge small classes.</strong> Each class is a namespace in every copy of the Worker. One class that
				routes by name is cheaper than several tiny ones.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Ask for a higher limit</strong> with the
				<a href={`${docs}/durable-objects/platform/limits/`}>limit increase form</a> if you still need it. This buys
				time, but cost still grows with PR count unless you change the pattern.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="compare">Comparison</h2>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th></th>
						<th>New namespaces per Preview</th>
						<th>Runs the branch's DO code</th>
						<th>State isolation</th>
						<th>Setup</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>A.</strong> Isolated Previews</td>
						<td><span class="badge warn">one per class</span></td>
						<td>Yes</td>
						<td>Separate namespace</td>
						<td>None</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>B.</strong> Shared host</td>
						<td><span class="badge good">0</span></td>
						<td>No (runs <code>main</code>)</td>
						<td>Key prefix in staging host</td>
						<td>Split Workers</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><strong>C.</strong> Shared host + isolated</td>
						<td><span class="badge good">0</span>, or one per class if DO code changed</td>
						<td>When it changed</td>
						<td>Key prefix, or separate namespace</td>
						<td>Split Workers + CI path filter</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Separate Worker per PR</td>
						<td><span class="badge bad">one per class</span></td>
						<td>Yes</td>
						<td>Separate namespace</td>
						<td>Manual cleanup</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Which should I pick?</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>A few open PRs at a time, or Durable Object code changes in most PRs:</strong> option A, and delete
				Previews when PRs close.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Many open PRs:</strong> option C. Use option B if you don't need isolated Durable Object testing
				per PR.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="setup">Set up the shared host</h2>
		<p>
			This walks through <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples/02-shared-host`}><code>examples/02-shared-host</code></a>.
			The code below is pulled straight from the repository.
		</p>

		<h3>1. Move the classes into a host Worker</h3>
		<p>
			The host holds the classes and the migrations. Its <code>staging</code> environment deploys a second copy
			for Previews. Both copies should be deployed from <code>main</code>.
		</p>
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/host/wrangler.jsonc" code={hostConfig} lang="jsonc" />
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/host/src/index.js" code={hostIndex} lang="js" />

		<h3>2. Bind the app to the host</h3>
		<p>
			The app has no classes and no migrations. Production binds to <code>my-app-do</code>, and Previews bind to
			<code>my-app-do-staging</code>.
		</p>
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/app/wrangler.jsonc" code={appConfig} lang="jsonc" />

		<h3>3. Scope object names per Preview</h3>
		<p>
			Every Preview talks to the same staging namespace, so prefix object names with a scope. CI sets the scope to
			the PR number with <code>--var PREVIEW_SCOPE:pr-123</code>.
		</p>
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/app/src/index.js" code={appIndex} lang="js" />

		<h3>4. Add the isolated fallback (option C)</h3>
		<p>
			A second entry point re-exports the classes. A second config gives that Worker its own migrations and local
			bindings.
		</p>
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/app/src/isolated.js" code={isolatedIndex} lang="js" />
		<CodeFile path="examples/02-shared-host/app/wrangler.isolated.jsonc" code={isolatedConfig} lang="jsonc" />

		<h3>5. Choose the Preview type in CI</h3>
		<p>
			This GitHub Actions workflow runs a shared Preview by default and an isolated one when <code>host/src</code>
			changed. It deletes both when the PR closes. A matching
			<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/ci/deploy.yml`}>deploy workflow</a> deploys the hosts and the app from
			<code>main</code>.
		</p>
		<CodeFile path="examples/ci/preview.yml" code={previewWorkflow} lang="yaml" />

		<h2 id="measured">What we measured</h2>
		<p>
			We deployed the examples in this repository and counted namespaces with the
			<a href={`${docs}/api/resources/durable_objects/subresources/namespaces/methods/list/`}>namespaces API</a>
			(<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/scripts/count-namespaces.sh`}><code>count-namespaces.sh</code></a>)
			after each step. Each Worker has 2 classes, <code>Counter</code> and <code>Chat</code>. Nothing ever calls
			<code>Chat</code>.
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Step</th>
						<th>New namespaces</th>
						<th>Created</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td>Deploy a Worker that defines 2 classes</td>
						<td>+2</td>
						<td><code>my-app_Counter</code>, <code>my-app_Chat</code></td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>wrangler preview --name pr-1</code>, then <code>pr-2</code></td>
						<td>+2 each</td>
						<td><code>my-app_pr-1_Counter</code>, <code>my-app_pr-1_Chat</code>, …</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Preview bindings pointed at production with <code>script_name</code> (same Worker)</td>
						<td>+2</td>
						<td>Still a separate namespace per class</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td><code>wrangler preview delete</code></td>
						<td>−2</td>
						<td>Freed right away</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Deploy host (production + staging) and app</td>
						<td>+4</td>
						<td><code>my-app-do_*</code>, <code>my-app-do-staging_*</code>. The app adds none.</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>3 Previews of the app Worker</td>
						<td><strong>0</strong></td>
						<td>All bind to the staging host</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Version URL of the app Worker (<code>versions upload --preview-alias</code>)</td>
						<td><strong>0</strong></td>
						<td>URL generated, because the app defines no Durable Object</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Isolated Preview (<code>--config wrangler.isolated.jsonc</code>) with no prior deploy</td>
						<td>+2</td>
						<td>Worker created automatically. Only <code>my-app-isolated_pr-6_*</code>, no production namespaces.</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			At runtime, we called <code>/count</code> twice on production, two shared Previews and the isolated Preview.
			Each returned <code>1</code> then <code>2</code> under its own scope. Calls across Workers through
			<code>script_name</code> worked, and the scopes didn't interfere.
		</p>
		<p class="muted">
			Tested with Wrangler 4.164.0 on November 17, 2026. <code>wrangler preview</code> is in open beta, so behavior
			may change. Re-run the examples to confirm on your account.
		</p>

		<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

		<h3>Can a Preview of the app bind to a Preview of the host?</h3>
		<p>
			Not today, as far as we know. We didn't find a way to do it.
			<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/resources/#service-bindings`}>Service bindings from a Preview</a> call the
			bound Worker's production deployment. Our examples assume Durable Object <code>script_name</code> bindings
			behave the same way. That's why option C bundles the classes into the app instead.
		</p>

		<h3>Does a Preview need the classes if the branch doesn't touch Durable Objects?</h3>
		<p>
			With option A, yes. The classes and migrations are part of the Worker, so every Preview gets them. To skip
			them, the Preview has to come from a Worker that doesn't define them, which is option B.
		</p>

		<h3>Does deleting the app Preview delete its data?</h3>
		<p>
			With option A, yes: the namespace and its storage are deleted with the Preview. With option B, no. The data
			lives in the staging host under the Preview's scope. Expire it with
			<a href={`${docs}/durable-objects/api/alarms/`}>alarms</a>, or call
			<code>ctx.storage.deleteAll()</code> from a cleanup job.
		</p>

		<h3>What about Containers?</h3>
		<p>
			<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/resources/#containers`}>Containers</a> are backed by Durable Objects, so
			each Preview gets its own namespace and container app. The same counting applies. We haven't tested the shared
			host pattern with Containers.
		</p>

		<footer>
			<p>
				Community example, not official Cloudflare documentation. For the source of truth, see the
				<a href={`${docs}/workers/previews/`}>Previews docs</a> and
				<a href={`${docs}/durable-objects/platform/limits/`}>Durable Objects limits</a>.
			</p>
			<p><a href={repo}>Source and runnable examples on GitHub</a> · MIT license</p>
		</footer>
	</main>
</Base>
